Pure recursion over fixed-size value-typed `[64]i64` locals — no `[]T` slice,
no heap allocation anywhere in this program. The thing under test is the
goroutine's fixed stack budget (`runtime/sched/`'s `stack_size`), not rooting.

CORRECTION: the original text here said "no loop anywhere". That was false —
`descend` contains THREE explicit `while` loops (initialising `pad`, then
summing it in either the base case or the recursive case), each executed
WIDTH=64 times on essentially every one of the 1001 calls `descend(1000)`
makes. Those loops are ordinary (not `@nosplit`) code, so codegen plants a
real `bit_rt_safepoint` on every one of their back-edges (runtime/ABI.md §4),
and that — not anything related to the recursion itself or a stack-growth
check — is what drives this program's collection count far above every other
straight-line marker in this corpus.

Measured, aarch64-macos: 5 runs of the real DEPTH=1000/WIDTH=64 build under
`BIT_GC=stress` gave 129011, 129011, 129437, 130017, 129985 (spread ~0.8%,
consistent with #4160's note that ambient counts move run to run under real
scheduler timing). Confirmed the loop mechanism directly rather than just
asserting it: a WIDTH=8 scratch build measured ~16215-16222 (down from
~129000, tracking the ~8x fewer loop iterations); a DEPTH=0 scratch build
(one call, base case only — 2 loop executions of 64 iterations each) measured
EXACTLY 229 on 3/3 runs, which is stress.bit's documented ~101 scheduler-loop
floor plus exactly 2*64=128 — an exact match with no residual to explain.

None of this changes what the program actually proves: `pad` is a stack-local
fixed-size array, never a GC-managed heap object, so no ambient collection —
however dense — can move or invalidate it, and the checksum this program
prints already catches stack corruption if the byte budget is ever exceeded.
The corpus-wide collection-count proof is still the wrong thing to run this
program through, because its huge count is real but comes entirely from loop
back-edges that have nothing to do with rooting, not from the program having
"legitimately zero" safepoints.
